The library is small (a tritanopia colour-blindness simulation), but getting byte-identical results hinged on three details in [38;5;10mc_src/src/lib.c[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mMixed float/double arithmetic.[22m The gamma functions store [38;5;10mfloat[0m but compute in [38;5;10mdouble[0m, because the literals ([38;5;10m0.04045[0m, [38;5;10m1.055[0m, [38;5;10m12.92[0m, [38;5;10m2.4[0m) are doubles and [38;5;10mpow[0m is the double version. Each rounding step is reproduced explicitly:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mfn remove_gamma_channel(c: f32) -> f32 {
    let c = c as f64;
    if c > 0.04045 {
        (unsafe { pow((c + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4) }) as f32
    } else {
        (c / 12.92) as f32
    }
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I call libm's [38;5;10mpow[0m directly rather than [38;5;10mf64::powf[0m so both builds use the identical implementation. The matrix in [38;5;10mTritanopia()[0m, by contrast, is all [38;5;10mf[0m-suffixed literals, so that stays in [38;5;10mf32[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mUndefined behaviour that must be preserved.[22m [38;5;10mcbDenorm[0m casts a [38;5;10mfloat[0m straight to [38;5;10munsigned char[0m, and out-of-range values genuinely occur — the tritanopia matrix pushes channels outside [38;5;10m[0, 1][0m, e.g. pure blue drives red to [38;5;10m-0.127[0m, which scales to [38;5;10m-419[0m. In C that cast is UB; on x86-64 it compiles to a truncating 32-bit convert followed by reading the low byte. Rust's [38;5;10mas u8[0m would instead saturate (clamping to 0 or 255), giving different bytes, so I emulate the hardware behaviour:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mlet scaled = c * 255.0f32 + 0.5f32;
(scaled.trunc() as i32) as c_uchar
